## What is a daemon (service ending in "d")

A **daemon** is a background program with no user interface — it just runs continuously, waiting to respond to events (connections, requests, scheduled triggers). The `d` suffix (`nessusd`, `sshd`, `crond`) stands for "daemon."

**Why daemons are used:**
- Services need to be always-on and ready, not manually launched each time
- They keep running after you log out/close your terminal
- Multiple users/processes can talk to one shared daemon (e.g. `dockerd` manages all containers for everyone)
- Efficient — mostly idle until triggered by an actual event

This is why `sudo systemctl start nessusd` is required before the web UI at `:8834` works — the UI is just a frontend; the daemon is the actual engine doing the work.

