## SNMP enumeration — full explanation

**What SNMP is:** Simple Network Management Protocol — used to remotely monitor/manage network devices. Runs on **UDP port 161**.

**What a "community string" is:** basically a shared password (just a text string) instead of a real username+password login. Defaults are almost always:
- `public` → read-only access
- `private` → read-write access (far more dangerous if left default)

**Steps:**
```
sudo apt install snmp onesixtyone -y
sudo nmap -sU -p 161 <target-IP>                          # confirm SNMP is open
onesixtyone <target-IP> public                             # test/guess community string
snmpwalk -v1 -c public <target-IP>                         # full enumeration dump
snmpwalk -v1 -c public <target-IP> 1.3.6.1.2.1.1            # system info branch only
```

**What the leaked info enables:**
- System description/version → attacker searches matching CVEs/exploits
- Network interfaces/routing → maps internal network, finds pivot targets
- Running processes/software → matches to known exploits
- Usernames (esp. Windows SNMP) → direct input for Hydra brute-forcing
- `private` string → read-write access, can actually reconfigure the device

**Viva one-liner:** *"SNMP enumeration is a reconnaissance step — it doesn't grant access directly, but leaked system/network details let an attacker build a targeted attack instead of guessing blindly."*

